ent as separate integers.\"\n\nSo I should split each argument by whitespace, then parse each token.\n\nNow let me12 the implementation.\n\nOne more thing: the rule says \"literal-string formats\" for println!/eprintln!. So:\n- `println!(\"ra\")` ✓\n- `println!(\"{}\", s)` ✗\n\nThis means each op needs its own println! call with the literal string.\n\nLet me write the code using helper functions called from sort3 and sort5, and radix for larger.\n\nActually, let me think about the radix sort more carefully.\n\nInput: [3, 1, 4, 1, 5] - wait, no duplicates allowed.\nInput: [3, 1, 4, 5, 2]\nSorted: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nIndices: [2, 0, 3, 4, 1] (3→2, 1→0, 4→3, 5→4, 2→1)\n\nNow radix sort by bits. Max bits for 5 elements: ceil(log2(5)) = 3.\n\nBit 0 (least significant):\n- Index 2 (010): bit 0 = 0 → pb\n- Index 0 (000): bit 0 = 0 → pb  \n- Index 3 (011): bit 0 = 1 → ra\n- Index 4 (100): bit 0 = 0 → pb (wait, this is after ra... let me redo)\n\nAfter pb of 2: A=[0,3,4,1], B=[2]\nAfter pb of 0: A=[3,4,1], B=[0,2]\nAfter ra of 3: A=[4,1,3], B=[0,2]\nAfter pb of 4 (index 4, bit 0 = 0)... but wait, we moved on to checking index 4. Bit 0 of 4 = 0 → pb. A=[1,3], B=[4,0,2]\nAfter checking index 1 (bit 0 = 1) → ra: A=[3,1], B=[4,0,2]\nHmm, but we should have checked all 5 elements. But we pushed 3, so A now has 2 elements.\n\nWait, the algorithm loops `size` times. Let me redo this.\n\nsize = 5 (number of elements)\nFor i in 0..5:\n  i=0: top is index 2 (010), bit 0 = 0 → pb. A=[0,3,4,1], B=[2]\n  i=1: top is index 0 (000), bit 0 = 0 → pb. A=[3,4,1], B=[0,2]\n  i=2: top is index 3 (011), bit 0 = 1 → ra. A=[4,1,3], B=[0,2]\n  i=3: top is index 4 (100), bit 0 = 0 → pb. A=[1,3], B=[4,0,2]\n  i=4: top is index 1 (001), bit 0 = 1 → ra. A=[3,1], B=[4,0,2]\n\nThen pa everything back:\npa: A=[4,3,1], B=[0,2]\npa: A=[0,4,3,1], B=[2]\npa: A=[2,0,4,3,1], B=[]\n\nAfter bit 0, the elements with bit 0 = 0 end up in front (relative order reversed), and bit 1 elements end up in back.\n\nBit 1:\n  i=