e(0); b.push(val); println!(\"rb\"); } }\nfn rr(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let did_a = a.len() >= 2;\n    let did_b = b.len() >= 2;\n    if did_a { let val = a.remove(0); a.push(val); }\n    if did_b { let val = b.remove(0); b.push(val); }\n    if did_a || did_b { println!(\"rr\"); }\n}\nfn rra(a: &mut Vec<i32>) { if a.len() >= 2 { let val = a.pop().unwrap(); a.insert(0, val); println!(\"rra\"); } }\nfn rrb(b: &mut Vec<i32>) { if b.len() >= 2 { let val = b.pop().unwrap(); b.insert(0, val); println!(\"rrb\"); } }\nfn rrr(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let did_a = a.len() >= 2;\n    let did_b = b.len() >= 2;\n    if did_a { let val = a.pop().unwrap(); a.insert(0, val); }\n    if did_b { let val = b.pop().unwrap(); b.insert(0, val); }\n    if did_a || did_b { println!(\"rrr\"); }\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the spec says for rr/ss/rrr: \"ra and rb together\". If only one works, should I print rr anyway? Hmm, I think the convention is to only print if both work, or at least one. Let me just be safe and print if either does something.\n\nActually re-reading: \"ra and rb together\" suggests it does ra and rb. But what if one stack has < 2 elements? I think it should still do what it can and print the combined instruction. So if one does nothing and the other works, still print.\n\nOK let me just code the full implementation now.\n\nI also need to worry about the static scan. Let me think about what pattern the scan might look for...\n\nThe scan strips comments and string literals, then checks for banned patterns. So:\n- `format!` would be flagged\n- `write!` would be flagged\n- `print!` would be flagged (but `println!` is OK)\n- `VecDeque` would be flagged\n- `.sort(` would be flagged (but not `.sort` in a10, since that's16)\n- `unsafe` would be flagged\n- `static mut` would be flagged\n\nI need to be careful about these patterns appearing in variable names or function names. For instance, I shouldn't name a variable `format` or call anything that con