cular order)\n2. Insert from B back to A in optimal order\n\nBut that's complex to implement.\n\nOr, a \"quick sort\" approach using the median of 5 as pivot.\n\nYou know what,16 just go with radix sort and optimize later if needed. The16 constraints are16: ≤1500 for 100, ≤11500 for 500. And radix sort should get close to those limits.\n\nLet me also think about another common push_swap algorithm: \"cost-based greedy insertion.\"\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. While A has > 3 elements: pb 2 at a time (awn to B in descending order or some order)\n2. Sort the last 3 in A\n3. While B not empty: find the cheapest element in B to insert into A, rotate A to the right position, pa\n\nFor step 3, the \"cost\" for each B element is: the number of rotations needed in B to bring it to top + the number of rotations needed in A to put it in the right place. Try both directions (ra and rra) for both stacks, pick the cheapest.\n\nThis can produce very good results, often well below the passing floors.\n\nBut it's more complex to implement. Let me start with radix sort and see.\n\nOK, I am now going to actually write the code. No more deliberation.\n\nLet me also think about memory. Vec<i32> for up to 500 elements is 2000 bytes. Totally fine.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\n```rust\nuse std::process::exit;\nuse std::env::args;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut a: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    \n    // Parse all arguments\n    let arg_iter = args().skip(1);\n    let args_vec: Vec<String> = arg_iter.collect();\n    \n    if args_vec.is_empty() {\n        exit(0);\n    }\n    \n    for arg in &args_vec {\n        let tokens: Vec<&str> = arg.split_whitespace().collect();\n        if tokens.is_empty() {\n            eprintln!(\"Error\");\n            exit(1);\n        }\n        for token in &tokens {\n            let val: i32 = match token.parse() {\n                Ok(v) => v,\n                Err(_) => {\n                    eprintln!(\"Error\");\n                    exit(1);\n                }\n           