t doesn't make sense for output.\n    \n    // The operations are printed. The values in a change. I need to06 sure\n    // the values in a end up sorted.\n    \n    // I think the way this works is: we tag each value with its index\n    // and use the index to decide where it goes, but the actual value\n    // moves with it.\n}\n```\n\nSo the algorithm is:\n1. Compute indices (position in sorted order) for each value\n2.acci radix sort treating the \"current value\" as the index - and when a value moves from A to B and back, it's sorted based on its index bits.\n\nBut my implementation won't literally replace values with indices. I'll store the index mapping in a separate array and consult it when deciding whether to pb or ra.\n\nWait, but this changes over time as elements move. Let me think...\n\nStack A at start: [3, 1, 4, 5, 2]\nIndices: [2, 0, 3, 4, 1]\n\nBit 0:\nCheck top of A: value is 3, index is 2. Bit 0 of index 2 = 0 → pb\nA now: [1, 4, 5, 2], B: [3]\n\nCheck top of A: value is 1, index is 0. Bit 0 = 0 → pb\nA: [4, 5, 2], B: [1, 3]\n\nCheck top of A: value is 4, index is 3. Bit 0 = 1 → ra\nA: [5, 2, 4], B: [1, 3]\n\nCheck top: value is 5, index is 4. Bit 0 = 0 → pb\nA: [2, 4], B: [5, 1, 3]\n\nCheck top: value is 2, index is 1. Bit 0 = 1 → ra\nA: [4, 2], B: [5, 1, 3]\n\nThen pa all back.\n\nBut how do I look up the index of a value? I need a mapping from value to index.\n\nYes, that's the right approach. I'll create a Vec<(i32, u32)> where the u32 is the position-index.\n\nLet me code it:\n\n```rust\nfn compute_indices(values: &[i32]) -> Vec<(i32, u32)> {\n    // Sort values to get order\n    let mut sorted = values.to_vec();\n    // insertion sort\n    for i in 1..sorted.len() {\n        let mut j = i;\n        while j > 0 && sorted[j - 1] > sorted[j] {\n            sorted.swap(j - 1, j);\n            j -= 1;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    let mut result = Vec::with_capacity(values.len());\n    for &v in values {\n        let pos = sorted.iter().position(|&x| x == v).unwrap() as u32;\n        result.push((v, pos));\n    }\n    result\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but this has O(n^2) for the position lookup. For n up to 500, this is fine. 500^2 = 250,000, which is trivial.\n\nBut wait, I can't use `sorted.iter().position()`. Actually, I can use `.iter().position()` - it's a method on Iterator returned by Vec::iter(), not a specific sort helper. Let me check... yes, `iter()` and `position()` are standard iterator methods, not banned.\n\nActually, I should double check - the banned list is very specific about sort helpers. `position` is not listed as banned. And `iter()` is listed as allowed.\n\nOK, let me go ahead and write the full implementation now.\n\nLet me also handle the case where there are duplicate values: I need toacci error. But `position` finds the first occurrence. If there are duplicates, the same value would appear with the same index, and the sorted list would have duplicates at adjacent positions. I'd need to handle this properly: for each value in the input, find its position and thenremove it from sorted so that the next duplicate gets the next position.\n\nActually wait, duplicates are not allowed. The spec says \"Duplicates are not allowed.\" So this is a non-issue.\n\nLet me also think about what happens with negative numbers. The radix sort works on indices (0 to n-1), which are always non-negative. So negative values are fine - the index is just their position in sorted order.\n\nNow let me write the full implementation. I'll use a simple, clean approach:\n\nFor 2 elements:\n- If not sorted: sa\n\nFor 3 elements:\n-acci hardcoded logic for each permutation\n\nFor 5 elements:\n- Push smallest 2 to B with careful rotations\n- Sort 3 in A\n- Insert from B back optimally\n\nFor >5 elements:\n- Radix sort\n\nWait, for the 5-element case, I need to think more carefully about how to optimally sort it.\n\nClassic push_swap for 5:\n1. Find the two smallest (or one of several approaches)\n2. But a common15 is:\n   - p