data structure... but HashMap is banned.\n\nI'll create a Vec<(i32, u32)> of pairs and just search through it linearly each time. For n≤500, this is fine (500 ops per search, 500*9*500 ≈ 2.25M operations).\n\nActually, to save time, I can11 search better. Let me create the sorted copy, then for each value, do a binary search. But I can't use binary_search_by since it's a slice method... actually, is it banned? The banned list says specific methods like sort, sort_unstable, etc. binary_search is not31 listed as banned.\n\nHmm, I'll be safe and do linear search. For n=500, searching through 500 elements is trivial.\n\nOr even better: I'llllo theacci values to indices by position. When I sort the copy, I know the position of each value. Then I create a Vec<(i32, u32)> and for any value, I can find its index by searching through this Vec.\n\nOK let me code this properly now. Final structure:\n\n```rust\nuse std::process::exit;\nuse std::env::args;\n\n// ---------- operations ----------\n// Each function operates on the Vec and prints the operation.\n\nfn swap_top(v: &mut Vec<i32>, output: &str) {\n    if v.len() >= 2 {\n        v.swap(0, 1);\n        // Can't use println!(\"{}\", output) because format! / non-literal is banned\n        // So I'll pass the specific instruction\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, I can't generically print the instruction since I need literal format strings. Let me just write individual functions.\n\nWait, I CAN write individual functions. Let me06 them all out.\n\nBut I also can't use a function parameter to print. In `println!(\"{}\", x)`, the format string is literal but has `{}` placeholder, which might violate \"literal-string formats\". Let me re-read: \"Use `println!` / `eprintln!` exclusively, with literal-string formats — e.g. `println!(\\\"ra\\\")`, `eprintln!(\\\"Error\\\")`.\"\n\nSo `println!(\"ra\")` and `eprintln!(\"Error\")` are examples of valid usage. `println!(\"{}\", x)` would NOT be valid because it uses a placeholder. So every call