rything back at once.\n\nBut this doesn't change the total count - we still need to iterate each bit for each element and then push back. It just changes when the push-back happens.\n\nActually, I can save some operations: when checking each element in A, if the top has the correct bit,16 to B. Otherwise ra. But ra effectively rotates A, so the24 elements that were pushed to B come back later.\n\nHmm,16 think about32 approach where I don't push back after each bit. Instead:\n1. For each bit from 0 to max_bit:\n   a.awn the 0-bit elements to B\n   b.wn back for the next bit\nBut this is exactly what I described.\n\nOK, let me try another approach: sort all elements, not one bit at a time, but32 sort.\n\nActually, maybe a better algorithm would give better results. Let me consider a \"chunked insertion\" approach:\n\n1. Push most elements to B (in a particular order)\n2. Insert from B back to A in optimal order\n\nBut that's complex to implement.\n\nOr, a \"quick sort\" approach using the median of 5 as pivot.\n\nYou know what,16 just go with radix sort and optimize later if needed. The16 constraints are16: ≤1500 for 100, ≤11500 for 500. And radix sort should get close to those limits.\n\nLet me also think about another common push_swap algorithm: \"cost-based greedy insertion.\"\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. While A has > 3 elements: pb 2 at a time (awn to B in descending order or some order)\n2. Sort the last 3 in A\n3. While B not empty: find the cheapest element in B to insert into A, rotate A to the right position, pa\n\nFor step 3, the \"cost\" for each B element is: the number of rotations needed in B to bring it to top + the number of rotations needed in A to put it in the right place. Try both directions (ra and rra) for both stacks, pick the cheapest.\n\nThis can produce very good results, often well below the passing floors.\n\nBut it's more complex to implement. Let me start with radix sort and see.\n\nOK, I am now going to actually write the code. No more deliberation.\n\nLet me also think about memory. Vec<i32> for up to 500 elements is 2000 bytes. Totally fine.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\n```rust\nuse std::process::exit;\nuse std::env::args;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut a: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    \n    // Parse all arguments\n    let arg_iter = args().skip(1);\n    let args_vec: Vec<String> = arg_iter.collect();\n    \n    if args_vec.is_empty() {\n        exit(0);\n    }\n    \n    for arg in &args_vec {\n        let tokens: Vec<&str> = arg.split_whitespace().collect();\n        if tokens.is_empty() {\n            eprintln!(\"Error\");\n            exit(1);\n        }\n        for token in &tokens {\n            let val: i32 = match token.parse() {\n                Ok(v) => v,\n                Err(_) => {\n                    eprintln!(\"Error\");\n                    exit(1);\n                }\n            };\n            for &x in &a {\n                if x == val {\n                    eprintln!(\"Error\");\n                    exit(1);\n                }\n            }\n            a.push(val);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    if a.is_empty() {\n        exit(0);\n    }\n    \n    // Check if already sorted\n    let mut sorted = true;\n    for i in 1..a.len() {\n        if a[i - 1] > a[i] {\n            sorted = false;\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    if sorted {\n        exit(0);\n    }\n    \n    let mut b: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    let n = a.len();\n    \n    if n == 1 {\n        exit(0);\n    } else if n == 2 {\n        if a[0] > a[1] {\n            sa(&mut a);\n        }\n    } else if n == 3 {\n        sort_three(&mut a);\n    } else if n == 5 {\n        sort_five(&mut a, &mut b);\n    } else {\n        radix_sort(&mut a, &mut b);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, I should be careful. I used `token.parse()` which returns `Result<i32, ParseIntError>`. The `parse::<i32>()` method is allowed. Let me be more explicit.\n\nAlso, I need to define all the op functions before main, or at least declare them.\n\nLet me think about the function signatures:\n\n```rust\nfn sa(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if a.len() >= 2 {\n        a.swap(0, 1);\n        println!(\"sa\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn sb(b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if b.len() >= 2 {\n        b.swap(0, 1);\n        println!(\"sb\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn ss(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let act_a = a.len() >= 2;\n    let act_b = b.len() >= 2;\n    if act_a { a.swap(0, 1); }\n    if act_b { b.swap(0, 1); }\n    if act_a || act_b { println!(\"ss\"); }\n}\n\nfn pa(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if !b.is_empty() {\n        a.insert(0, b.remove(0));\n        println!(\"pa\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn pb(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if !a.is_empty() {\n        b.insert(0, a.remove(0));\n        println!(\"pb\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn ra(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if a.len() >= 2 {\n        let val = a.remove(0);\n        a.push(val);\n        println!(\"ra\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn rb(b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if b.len() >= 2 {\n        let val = b.remove(0);\n        b.push(val);\n        println!(\"rb\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn rr(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let act_a = a.len() >= 2;\n    let act_b = b.len() >= 2;\n    if act_a { let val = a.remove(0); a.push(val); }\n    if act_b { let val = b.remove(0); b.push(val); }\n    if act_a || act_b { println!(\"rr\"); }\n}\n\nfn rra(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if a.len() >= 2 {\n        let val = a.pop().unwrap();\n        a.insert(0, val);\n        println!(\"rra\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn rrb(b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if b.len() >= 2 {\n        let val = b.pop().unwrap();\n        b.insert(0, val);\n        println!(\"rrb\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn rrr(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let act_a = a.len() >= 2;\n    let act_b = b.len() >= 2;\n    if act_a { let val = a.pop().unwrap(); a.insert(0, val); }\n    if act_b { let val = b.pop().unwrap(); b.insert(0, val); }\n    if act_a || act_b { println!(\"rrr\"); }\n}\n```\n\nWait, I used `.pop().unwrap()` in rra and rrb. The `.pop()` returns an Option<i32>. I know the Vec has at least 1 element (since I checked len >= 2), so unwrap() is safe here. This won't panic.\n\nFor rr and rrr, I use `let val = ...` inside the if block. The24 `val` variable12 inside the if scope, so there's no conflict.\n\nActually wait, in rr: `if act_a { let val = a.remove(0); a.push(val); }` - this would be fine.\n\nBut what about rra: `a.len() >= 2`, so pop returns Some(val). unwrap() is safe.\n\nHmm, but there's a minor issue: in rra, after `a.len() >= 2` check, a has at least 2 elements, so a.pop() definitely returns Some. But `pop().unwrap()` could be considered a \"not-best practice\". However, since I know pop will succeed, it's correct. The prompt says \"no `unwrap()` on values that may legitimately be `None`\". Since pop() on a non-empty Vec always returns Some, this is fine.\n\nOK, now let me write the sort functions:\n\n```rust\nfn sort_three(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let (x, y, z) = (a[0], a[1], a[2]);\n    \n    if x < y && y < z {\n        // Already sorted [1,2,3]\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    if x > y && y < z {\n        if x < z {\n            // [2,1,3] -> sa\n            sa(a);\n        } else {\n            // [3,1,2] -> ra\n            ra(a);\n        }\n    } else if x < y && y > z {\n        if x < z {\n            // [1,3,2] -> rra then sa\n            rra(a);\n            sa(a);\n        } else {\n            // [2,3,1] -> rra\n            rra(a);\n        }\n    } else {\n        // [3,2,1] -> sa then rra\n        sa(a);\n        rra(a);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, let me double check my cases:\n\nSorted ascending means a[0] < a[1] < a[2].\n\nCase [1,2,3]: a[0]=1, a[1]=2, a[2]=3. 1<2<3. Sorted.\n\nCase [1,3,2]: a[0]=1, a[1]=3, a[2]=2.\n  x=1, y=3, z=2. x<y, y>z, x<z. → rra, sa.\n  rra: A=[2,1,3]. sa: A=[1,2,3]. ✓\n\nCase [2,1,3]: a[0]=2, a[1]=1, a[2]=3.\n  x=2, y=1, z=3. x>y, y<z, x<z. → sa.\n  sa: A=[1,2,3]. ✓\n\nCase [2,3,1]: a[0]=2, a[1]=3, a[2]=1.\n  x=2, y=3, z=1. x<y, y>z, x>z. → rra.\n  rra: A=[1,2,3]. ✓\n\nCase [3,1,2]: a[0]=3, a[1]=1, a[2]=2.\n  x=3, y=1, z=2. x>y, y<z, x>z. → ra.\n  ra: A=[1,2,3]. ✓\n\nCase [3,2,1]: a[0]=3, a[1]=2, a[2]=1.\n  x=3, y=2, z=1. x>y, y>z. → sa, rra.\n  sa: A=[2,3,1]. rra: A=[1,2,3]. ✓\n\nAll 6 cases handled correctly.\n\nWait, do I need to call the functions? The sa, ra, rra functions both perform the operation AND print. But inside sort_three, I'm calling them directly. That's fine - they handle the printing.\n\nNow for sort_five:\n\n```rust\nfn sort_five(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    for _ in 0..2 {\n        let min_idx = find_min_index(a);\n        rotate_to_top_a(a, min_idx);\n        pb(a, b);\n    }\n    sort_three(a);\n    while !b.is_empty() {\n        pa(a, b);\n    }\n}\n\nfn find_min_index(v: &[i32]) -> usize {\n    let mut min_idx = 0;\n    for i in 1..v.len() {\n        if v[i] < v[min_idx] {\n            min_idx = i;\n        }\n    }\n    min_idx\n}\n\nfn rotate_to_top_a(a: &mut Vec<i32>, idx: usize) {\n    let n = a.len();\n    if idx <= n / 2 {\n        for _ in 0..idx {\n            ra(a);\n        }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(n - idx) {\n            rra(a);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis should handle 5 elements correctly. Let me verify with the examples I computed earlier. The algorithm:\n1. Find smallest, rotate to top, pb\n2. Find next smallest (now in remaining 4), rotate to top, pb\n3. Sort the 3\n4. Pa both back\n\nAfter step 3, A is sorted ascending (3 elements, smallest at top).\nAfter pa both back, A=[s, 2nd, m1, m2, m3] where s < 2nd < m1 < m2 < m3.\n\nThis is sorted. ✓\n\nBut there's a subtle issue: what if after push to B, the16 A are reversed? For instance, if A=[5,4,3,2,1]:\n- Find min: 1 at index 4, rra→[1,5,4,3,2], pb→A=[5,4,3,2], B=[1]\n- Find min (of [5,4,3,2]): 2 at index 3, rra→[2,5,4,3], pb→A=[5,4,3], B=[2,1]\n- sort3([5,4,3]): descending. sa→[4,5,3], rra→[3,4,5].\n- pa: A=[2,3,4,5], B=[1]\n- pa: A=[1,2,3,4,5]. Correct.\n\nAnother case: A=[1,5,2,4,3]\n- Find min: 1 at index 0, pb→A=[5,2,4,3], B=[1]\n- Find min (of [5,2,4,3]): 2 at index 1, ra→[2,4,3,5], pb→A=[4,3,5], B=[2,1]\n- sort3([4,3,5]): 4>3, 3<5? No wait, x=4, y=3, z=5. x>y, y<z, x<z → sa. \n  sa→[3,4,5]. Correct.\n- pa: A=[2,3,4,5], B=[1]\n- pa: A=[1,2,3,4,5]. Correct.\n\nSeems correct.\n\nNow for radix_sort:\n\n```rust\nfn radix_sort(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let n = a.len();\n    \n    // Compute indices\n    let indices = compute_indices(a);\n    \n    let mut max_bits = 0;\n    let mut tmp = n as u32;\n    while tmp > 0 {\n        max_bits += 1;\n        tmp >>= 1;\n    }\n    \n    for bit in 0..max_bits {\n        for _ in 0..n {\n            // Get index of current top of a\n            let val = a[0];\n            let idx = find_index(&indices, val);\n            if (idx >> bit) & 1 == 0 {\n                pb(a, b);\n            } else {\n                ra(a);\n            }\n        }\n        while !b.is_empty() {\n            pa(a, b);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn compute_indices(values: &[i32]) -> Vec<(i32, u32)> {\n    let len = values.len();\n    let mut sorted = Vec::with_capacity(len);\n    for &v in values {\n        sorted.push(v);\n    }\n    \n    // Insertion sort\n    for i in 1..sorted.len() {\n        let mut j = i;\n        while j > 0 && sorted[j - 1] > sorted[j] {\n            sorted.swap(j - 1, j);\n            j -= 1;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    let mut result = Vec::with_capacity(len);\n    for &v in values {\n        let mut pos: u32 = 0;\n        for (si, &sv) in sorted.iter().enumerate() {\n            if sv == v {\n                pos = si as u32;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        result.push((v, pos));\n    }\n    result\n}\n\nfn find_index(indices: &[(i32, u32)], val: i32) -> u32 {\n    for &(v, idx) in indices {\n        if v == val {\n            return idx;\n        }\n    }\n    0 // unreachable\n}\n```\n\nHmm, wait. After computing indices, the indices Vec is in the same order as the original a order. But as a changes (via ra, pb, etc.), the order in the indices Vec doesn't match.\n\nActually, I need a way to look up the index of any value at any time. Since values are unique, I can8 create a mapping of value → index. I'll create the indices Vec as a8 vector and search through it each time. This is O(n) per lookup, but with n≤500, it's fine.\n\nThe approach:\n- For each element in A, I need to know its index (position in sorted order)\n- I have a Vec<(i32, u32)> where I can search for the value and find its index\n- This Vec doesn't change - it's fixed\n\nSo:\n1. Create indices mapping: value → sorted position\n2. During radix sort, for each element checked, look up its index via linear search\n\nThis works because:\n- Values don't change\n- Values are unique\n- The mapping is fixed regardless of stack position\n\nLet me verify with a small example:\nA=[3,1,4,5,2]\nSorted: [1,2,3,4,5]\nIndices: [(3,2), (1,0), (4,3), (5,4), (2,1)]\n\nBit 0:\n  val=3, idx=2, bit=0 → pb. A=[1,4,5,2], B=[3]\n  val=1, idx=0, bit=0 → pb. A=[4,5,2], B=[1,3]\n  val=4, idx=3, bit=1 → ra. A=[5,2,4], B=[1,3]\n  val=5, idx=4, bit=0 → pb. A=[2,4], B=[5,1,3]\n  val=2, idx=1, bit=1 → ra. A=[4,2], B=[5,1,3]\npa → A=[5,4,2], B=[1,3]\npa → A=[1,5,4,2], B=[3]\npa → A=[3,1,5,4,2]\n\nBit 1:\n  val=3, idx=2, bit=1 → ra. A=[1,5,4,2,3]\n  val=1, idx=0, bit=0 → pb. A=[5,4,2,3], B=[1]\n  val=5, idx=4, bit=0 → pb. A=[4,2,3], B=[5,1]\n  val=4, idx=3, bit=1 → ra. A=[2,3,4], B=[5,1]\n  val=2, idx=1, bit=0 → pb. A=[3,4], B=[2,5,1]\npa → A=[2,3,4], B=[5,1]\npa → A=[5,2,3,4], B=[1]\npa → A=[1,5,2,3,4]\n\nBit 2:\n  val=1, idx=0, bit=0 → pb. A=[5,2,3,4], B=[1]\n  val=5, idx=4, bit=0 → pb. A=[2,3,4], B=[5,1]\n  val=2, idx=1, bit=0 → pb. A=[3,4], B=[2,5,1]\n  val=3, idx=2, bit=0 → pb. A=[4], B=[3,2,5,1]\n  val=4, idx=3, bit=1 → ra. A=[4], B=[3,2,5,1]\n  Wait, there's only 1 element in A (index 4, bit=1 → ra). But16 loops 5 times:\n  \n  Ah wait, n=5. But A has only 1 element after pushing 4 times. So the16 check:\n  \"for _ in 0..n\":\n    i=0: val=1, pb. A=[5,2,3,4], B=[1]\n    i=1: val=5, pb. A=[2,3,4], B=[5,1]\n    i=2: val=2, pb. A=[3,4], B=[2,5,1]\n    i=3: val=3, pb. A=[4], B=[3,2,5,1]\n    i=4: val=4, ra. A=[4], B=[3,2,5,1]\n    \n  Oops! On iteration i=4, A has only [4]. ra requires len>=2, so ra does nothing. But we already ran out of elements!\n\n  Hmm, this is a problem. The loop `for _ in 0..n` assumes there are n elements to process in A. But after pushing some to B, A has fewer elements. When A becomes empty but16 loop still continues, a[0] would panic.\n\n  But looking at my operations, ra doesn't remove elements from A, it just rotates. And pb removes from A and adds to B. So the total number of elements in play (A + B) is always n. The question is: how many are in A at each iteration?\n\n  After pushing i elements to B, A has n - i elements. For i < n, A will always have at least 1 element, so a[0] won't16.\n\n  But for i=n, A would be empty and a[0] would panic. But if ra is called when A has 1 element, it does nothing (len < 2). And if pb is called when A has 1 element, it moves that last element to B, and A becomes empty.\n\n  After A becomes empty, the next iteration of the loop tries a[0], which panics.\n\n  So the algorithm needs to handle this: stop when A is empty, or check A's length before accessing a[0].\n\n  Let me fix:\n\n  ```rust\n  for _ in 0..n {\n      if a.is_empty() {\n          break;\n      }\n      let val = a[0];\n      let idx = find_index(&indices, val);\n      if (idx >> bit) & 1 == 0 {\n          pb(a, b);\n      } else {\n          ra(a);\n      }\n  }\n  ```\n\n  This handles the case where A becomes empty mid-loop.\n\n  Let me re-trace with this fix:\n\n  Bit 2 (n=5):\n    i=0: val=1, pb. A=[5,2,3,4], B=[1]\n    i=1: val=5, pb. A=[2,3,4], B=[5,1]\n    i=2: val=2, pb. A=[3,4], B=[2,5,1]\n    i=3: val=3, pb. A=[4], B=[3,2,5,1]\n    i=4: val=4, ra (but ra needs len>=2, so does nothing). A=[4], B=[3,2,5,1]\n\n  Hmm, th